WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.639
Hi, I'm Purlin!

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:06.355
Today: how one model learns to work as a whole company, from

00:00:06.355 --> 00:00:07.593
a single shared reward.

00:00:09.808 --> 00:00:12.417
Copies of one model play a boss and its hires.

00:00:12.417 --> 00:00:15.549
Every word any of them writes is trained on the same number.

00:00:19.342 --> 00:00:22.284
The boss plans, hires specialists and writes each a role.

00:00:22.284 --> 00:00:24.049
They all share the same weights.

00:00:24.469 --> 00:00:26.508
The team works through a shared message board.

00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:32.268
The environment scores the team, and every agent's rewards are added into one total.

00:00:32.688 --> 00:00:36.496
That total becomes one advantage on every word every agent wrote.

00:00:36.496 --> 00:00:38.573
Nobody estimates who caused the win.

00:00:39.858 --> 00:00:40.982
Here is the formula.

00:00:41.858 --> 00:00:43.277
R is the whole team's total.

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:47.297
It is compared with sixteen other attempts at the task.

00:00:47.952 --> 00:00:50.659
If every attempt scored the same, the group is dropped.

00:00:52.275 --> 00:00:53.909
A team spends more tokens.

00:00:53.909 --> 00:00:56.196
To compare time fairly, replay the log.

00:00:56.616 --> 00:00:58.178
Each agent keeps its own clock.

00:00:59.769 --> 00:01:01.433
Tool calls add their measured time.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.427
Waiting for a message costs nothing.

00:01:05.187 --> 00:01:07.266
A message pulls the reader's clock forward.

00:01:07.896 --> 00:01:10.032
The task takes as long as the largest clock.

00:01:11.842 --> 00:01:15.892
At matched tokens, one long chain of thought took a hundred and

00:01:15.892 --> 00:01:17.579
twenty-one minutes per proof problem.

00:01:17.579 --> 00:01:18.929
The organisation took thirty-eight.

00:01:20.383 --> 00:01:24.334
Over training the boss starts hiring lead engineers, a middle layer absent

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:25.980
for the first eighty steps.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.577
Held-out tasks show the same rise, smaller.

00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:33.369
In safety tests, agents never take control from humans.

00:01:33.789 --> 00:01:37.343
But reporting problems to a human falls away over training.

00:01:37.343 --> 00:01:39.121
The report calls this insularity.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.551
One flat team reward can train an organisation.

00:01:43.551 --> 00:01:46.589
At matched tokens, what it mostly buys is speed.

00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:51.920
One model, one reward, sooner answers.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.034
Thanks for watching!

00:01:54.292 --> 00:01:56.353
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:56.353 --> 00:01:56.942
I'm Purlin.

00:01:56.942 --> 00:01:57.237
Bye!
